rustgis-grass-plugin (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A ribbon tab of its own, with groups for the database, the region, raster
    work and the vector tools, the last as menus by GRASS keyword.
  * Every installed module is offered. The forms of 64 vector modules are
    generated as geoprocessing tools, which is every tool a manifest holds,
    and all 1,172 modules the installation could describe are in a searchable
    tree in the panel. Run Module runs the chosen one on the chosen raster
    layer with GRASS's own defaults; the 357 that read a raster and write one
    are marked in the tree.
  * The module list comes from the installation itself, addons included, and
    the generator says how many modules it read, how many became tools and
    which ones it had to leave out.
  * Parameters RustGIS would refuse are left out and reported: a name with a
    digit in it (v.gsflow.segments's "width1") and a name the module declares
    twice (the same module does, four times over).

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 15:05:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The computational region comes from RustGIS's Analysis Environment: its
    extent becomes the region's bounds, clipped to the raster and aligned to
    its grid, and its cell size the resolution, converted to the raster's own
    units. The panel can set the extent to the map view or clear it, and
    carries a cell size of its own that wins over both.
  * r.slope.aspect is given -a whenever the region was narrowed. Without it the
    module puts the region back on the elevation map's own grid, and the cell
    size asked for is ignored with nothing said.
  * The panel reaches the GRASS database GRASS itself uses, found from GISDBASE
    or its settings file: projects and mapsets are listed from the file system,
    a mapset's maps when it is opened, and a chosen map is exported and added
    to the project. The export runs in a throwaway mapset, so the user's own
    mapsets are never locked. A button also puts the database in the browser's
    Folders, which is as near the Add Data menu as a plugin can get.
  * A GRASS warning wrapped over several lines is kept whole. It was cut at the
    first line, which left sentences such as "East-west resolution does not
    equal north-south resolution. The" in the run's messages.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 14:20:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First package. Five GRASS vector modules as RustGIS geoprocessing tools,
    with every form read from the installed GRASS rather than kept by hand.
  * Three raster modules as commands with a panel: slope, aspect and viewshed.
    A plugin tool can take no raster and make no host request, so these export
    the chosen layer, run the module and add the GeoTIFF back, inside the two
    minutes RustGIS allows a command.
  * Each run reports the projection it worked in, the computational region it
    set and every distinct GRASS warning with a count. The region is never
    left at its default: a fresh project's one-cell region silently drops
    features.
  * rustgis-grass-setup generates the manifest from the GRASS on this machine
    and registers the plugin for the user who runs it.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 13:55:00 +0100
